// src/std/core/loop/loop_iter.zen
// one construct. the compiler picks the variant by signature - the
// ordinary overload rule, with a closure's type being its full
// parameter list. no carve-out for closures, which is the whole
// reason Zen needs no `for` keyword and no second control-flow
// concept to go with it.
//
// LOOPS NEVER ALLOCATE. Every declaration here has no Alloc
// parameter, and by law 1 that is not a promise, it is the
// signature: no Alloc parameter, no allocation. The two words that
// can allocate live in loop_find.zen, take an Alloc, and are
// different NAMES rather than overloads - a generic `init: A` would
// swallow `alloc: Alloc` and they could not be told apart.
//
// What the no-allocation claim rests on, mechanically:
//   - a body is a non-escaping stack closure, inlined at the call
//     site, so there is no closure record to box
//   - index and acc thread BY VALUE, so no cell is heap-allocated
//     to hold them across passes
//   - a fold over a stack array is a plain C for-loop
// tests/bench/bench_loop.zen gates the last one at allocs_op: 0.
// If it is ever 1, an inliner regressed and the body is being
// boxed - that budget is deterministic, so it fails the build.
//
// EVERY DECLARATION HERE IS A SIGNATURE, not a definition, and that
// is a decision rather than an omission. A loop is the one thing
// that cannot be written in a language whose only control flow is
// `.match`, `.loop` and recursion: writing `loop` in terms of
// `loop` is circular, and writing it by recursion makes "compiles
// to a plain C for-loop" depend on a tail-call optimisation nobody
// gates. So the compiler lowers each overload below to a C loop and
// the budget is locked down rather than hoped for. Everything
// ABOVE the primitive - find, filter, map - is ordinary Zen written
// on top of it, which is what proves h.break is a general feature.

LoopHandle = std.core.loop.loop_handle
Range      = std.core.range
Res        = std.core.result
Map        = std.collections

// while true. h.break is the only way out.
loop*<T> = (body: (h: LoopHandle) ()) Res<T>

// while true, with iteration counter. one more parameter, and the
// counter starts at 0 and threads by value.
loop*<T> = (body: (h: LoopHandle, index: usize) ()) Res<T>

// while cond. tested BEFORE each pass, so a cond that is false to
// begin with runs the body zero times.
loop*<T> = (cond: () bool, body: (h: LoopHandle) ()) Res<T>

// ranged / collection iteration, with and without index. index is
// the pass count and value is the element: over Range(10, 13) they
// are different numbers, and a body that confuses them prints the
// same one twice.
loop*<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, body: (h: LoopHandle, index: usize, value: T) ()) Res<T>
loop*<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, body: (h: LoopHandle, value: T) ()) Res<T>

// fold: init seeds acc, acc threads through iterations, the loop
// evaluates to the final acc - or to h.break(value), which is what
// makes the two forms one thing rather than two. init is the answer
// when there is nothing to fold.
loop*<R: Range<T>, T, A> = (range: R, init: A, body: (h: LoopHandle, index: usize, value: T, acc: A) A) Res<A>

// key/value containers, and A DIFFERENT NAME rather than a seventh
// overload of loop. DESIGN.md writes this one as
//
//     loop*<K, V> = (map: Map<K, V>, body: (h, key: K, value: V) ())
//
// and that cannot resolve against the ranged-with-index form two
// declarations up. Both take two arguments; both take a
// three-parameter body; and the first parameter is generic in one
// and concrete in the other, which is exactly the case DESIGN.md's
// own overloading section rules out - "a generic parameter swallows
// a concrete one" - and which a call site therefore cannot tell
// apart. That is a declaration-site error, named at both
// declarations, not something a caller finds out later.
//
// The law forced `map` to be a name instead of an overload for the
// identical reason, and this is the same trade taken the same way:
// a Map hands out two things per pass and a Range hands out one, so
// they were never one word, and the honest name says which you get.
// Reported as a doc bug against DESIGN.md rather than resolved
// quietly here.
pairs*<K, V> = (map: Map<K, V>, body: (h: LoopHandle, key: K, value: V) ()) Res<()>
